\section{The Symbolic Mirage}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on $\geq$99\% of problems across all weight settings $\alpha$ (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting.

\looseness=-1 Empirically, confirmation bias extends beyond incorrect instances. On problems where SC selects the correct answer, constraints still disproportionately support the majority ($\text{BR}_\text{correct}$ $1.29$--$3.64\times$ across all six tested conditions, with 5/6 showing $\text{BR}_\text{correct} \geq \text{BR}_\text{wrong}$), confirming that self-extracted constraints track distributional frequency rather than logical validity. A permutation null model---randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts (1{,}000 permutations)---yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ across all tested conditions ($p > 0.7$), confirming that BR reflects structural coupling rather than an independent bias signal. The bias is inherent to the majority-vote geometry (Observation~1): under source-attribution scoring, BR $> 1$ follows from trace-count distributions, not extraction artifacts.

\looseness=-1 The pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.85\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero variable overlap across answer groups, per-problem average), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $n_\text{err}$: number of incorrect instances on which BR is computed. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; BR estimates for high-accuracy models ($n_\text{err} < 40$) have wider confidence intervals; Gemini ($n_\text{err} \approx 12$) should be interpreted with particular caution. Process-level similarity ($\kappa_{\text{proc}}$) ranges from $0.25$ (R1-Distill) to $0.79$ (gpt-4o/4.1/Gemini); see Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}.}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.98 & 49 & 3.85 & $-$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 76.96 & 47 & 2.65 & $-$1.96 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 34 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.05 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.70\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). Symbolic confirmation bias reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\label{sec:process-kappa}
\looseness=-1 Reasoning-process overlap exceeds answer-level correlation: on Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean process similarity (pairwise TF-IDF cosine; Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}) reaches 0.69, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$--$0.51$ across seeds). The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with process similarity ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}). Conditional correction analysis confirms the same pattern: cross-architecture NLI yields positive correction ratios while same-model SICA yields negative ones, with correction ratio correlating with combo gain ($\rho = 0.84$, $p = 0.014$; Appendix~\ref{app:cil}).

\looseness=-1 These patterns---universal BR $> 1$, structural equivalence to trace-count distributions, and process-level correlation exceeding answer-level agreement---follow from a structural property of self-extraction pipelines.

\smallskip\noindent
\textbf{Observation 1} \textit{(Argmax Preservation).}\;%
Let $t_1, \ldots, t_K \overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim} P_M(\cdot \mid q)$ with trace-determined answers $a_i = h(t_i)$ and $n_a = |\{i : a_i = a\}|$.
Under (i)~the degeneration condition $|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}), (ii)~source-attribution scoring where $\sigma(c,a) {=} 1$ iff $c$ originates from a trace producing $a$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}), and (iii)~approximately uniform constraint yield per trace within each answer group, SICA reduces to majority vote: $\arg\max_a \operatorname{score}(a) = \arg\max_a n_a$.

\smallskip\noindent
\textit{Empirical support.}\;%
Under degeneration, $\mathcal{C}^* \approx \mathcal{C}$ and the penalty term vanishes; source-attribution restricts each constraint's contribution to its originating answer; approximately uniform yield makes total weight proportional to $n_a$, preserving the argmax. SICA and majority vote select the same answer on over 90\% of problems across all tested conditions.

\smallskip\noindent
\textbf{Remark 1} \textit{(Information Ceiling).}\;%
If constraints are trace-determined ($c_i = f(t_i)$), the data processing inequality gives $I(C_{1:K};\, Y) \leq I(T_{1:K};\, Y)$---a standard result for any deterministic post-processing. The contribution is identifying the $M{\to}T{\to}A{\to}C$ Markov chain structure: under the degeneration condition of Observation~1 ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), constraints are fully determined by answers, yielding the tighter bound $I(C;\, Y) \leq I(A;\, Y)$ and the approximate conditional independence $C \approx\!\!\perp Y \mid A$ shown in Figure~\ref{fig:causal-dag}. This is an empirical conclusion holding under degeneration, not an unconditional structural claim.

\smallskip\noindent
\textbf{Remark 2.}\;%
Observation~1 assumes degeneration and source attribution. Under \emph{semantic} evaluation, the equivalence additionally requires \emph{answer alignment}: $P(c_i \text{ supports } a_i) \geq P(c_i \text{ supports } a')$ for $a' \neq a_i$, which is structurally motivated and empirically validated ($\mathrm{BR} \in [1.66,\, 4.97]$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}), but could fail if $f$ systematically extracted premises contradicting their source trace's conclusion.
Since Observation~1 shows SICA reduces to majority vote under degeneration, $\mathrm{BR} \geq 1$ follows from the trace-count geometry; the empirical BR consistently exceeds this floor ($1.66\times$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}), reflecting both base-rate effects and extraction alignment.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=0.8cm,
  nd/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=black!50,
             fill=gray!6, minimum height=5.5mm, minimum width=8mm,
             font=\small},
  bn/.style={nd, fill=red!10, draw=red!50, line width=0.7pt},
  ex/.style={nd, draw=gray!50, densely dashed, fill=white},
  >=Stealth,
  base/.style={->, semithick, black!50},
  leak/.style={->, line width=0.85pt, red!50},
  sub/.style={font=\scriptsize, text=gray!55},
]
\node[nd]             (M) {$M$};
\node[nd, right=of M] (T) {$T$};
\node[bn, right=of T] (A) {$A$};
\node[nd, right=of A] (C) {$C$};
\node[nd, right=of C] (S) {$S$};
\node[ex, above=0.65cm of A] (Y) {$Y$};

\node[sub, below=0.5mm of M] {Model};
\node[sub, below=0.5mm of T] {Trace};
\node[sub, below=0.5mm of A] {Answer};
\node[sub, below=0.5mm of C] {Constr.};
\node[sub, below=0.5mm of S] {SICA};
\node[sub, above=0.5mm of Y] {Truth};

\draw[base] (M) -- (T);
\draw[leak] (T) -- (A);
\draw[leak] (A) -- (C);
\draw[base] (C) -- (S);
\draw[base, bend right=22] (T) to (C);

\draw[densely dashed, gray!30, shorten >=1pt, shorten <=1pt] (Y) -- (A);

\node[font=\tiny, text=red!45, fill=white, inner sep=0.5pt]
  at ($(A)!0.5!(C) + (0, 0.27)$) {\textit{bottleneck}};

\node[draw=gray!30, rounded corners=1.5pt, fill=white,
      inner sep=2.5pt, font=\scriptsize, text=gray!50,
      right=0.2cm of Y]
  {$C \approx\!\!\perp Y \mid A$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Causal DAG of the self-extraction pipeline. All information about ground truth $Y$ reaching constraints $C$ is mediated by answer $A$. Approximate conditional independence ($C \approx\!\!\perp Y \mid A$) is empirically supported by ${>}90\%$ argmax agreement (Observation~1) but is not a formal consequence of the DPI bound (Remark~1).}
\label{fig:causal-dag}
\end{figure}

\looseness=-1 Since the bias holds for any model, task, and extraction function $f$, training-free remediation strategies face a structural limitation: methods that preserve extraction accuracy necessarily inherit the model's distributional prior.

\subsection{Why Remediation Fails}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free variants within the constraint-extraction paradigm, spanning four categories, produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). The pattern reveals a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off: methods that successfully break trace correlation simultaneously destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy fail to achieve independence.

\looseness=-1 Four representative failure modes illustrate this trade-off. \emph{Debate} ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.6$\,pp) breaks correlation but collapses accuracy. \emph{Cross-model extraction} decreases $\kappa$ slightly but $\Delta \approx 0$, because same-family models share systematic biases (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). \emph{Contrastive prompting} produces imbalanced output (94 pro vs.\ 65 con), indicating confirmation bias transfers to constraint generation. \emph{Iterative refinement} reduces surface duplication but refined constraints still align with the majority answer.

\looseness=-1 All non-debate strategies cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling across three families: regardless of prompting strategy, self-extraction cannot produce constraints independent of the model's distributional prior. The oracle ceiling ($+29.90$\,pp with gold constraints) proves the mechanism is sound; self-extraction captures only ${\sim}$12\%. No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}), suggesting symbolic confirmation bias is intrinsic to self-referential constraint generation within the extraction-aggregation paradigm.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_interventions_compact.pdf}
\caption{Twenty-three of 27 training-free strategies across four categories (four additional strategies tested but omitted for clarity). All produce neutral-to-negative $\Delta$ vs.\ SC. Grey band: $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Diagnostic Framework}
\label{sec:diagnostic-framework}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

\looseness=-1 Three zero-cost diagnostics predict the outcome of any self-extraction-based aggregation method from a $K = 12$ pilot run: (1) the degenerate-regime indicator $C_0 < 5\%$ (SICA reduces to SC); (2) the diversity floor $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.30$ (insufficient vote diversity for any aggregation gain); (3) the bias ratio BR combined with SC regime (Type~A: SC $\geq 60\%$ with BR $> 1$ predicts confirmation-bias degradation; Type~C: SC $\leq 40\%$ predicts error amplification). These boundaries correctly characterize the failure mode across all 60 tested conditions (16 main configurations, 27 remediation variants on Mistral-7B${\times}$FOLIO, and 17 analytical conditions) within the self-extraction paradigm. The framework's practical value lies in failure-mode diagnosis---degenerate, diversity-limited, or confirmation-biased---from a $K{=}12$ pilot run, enabling practitioners to rule out self-extraction before full deployment. Conversely, when diagnostics detect sufficient capability gap (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}), the framework correctly predicts positive gain ($+6.67$\,pp for Mistral-7B on ProofWriter).

\looseness=-1 Decision boundaries ($C_0 < 5\%$, $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.30$, SC $< 40\%$) were derived from Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-14B. We evaluate prospectively on two held-out generators: LLaMA-3.1-8B (different family) on FOLIO, where the framework predicts Type~A bias and the observed $\Delta = -4.41$\,pp confirms; and Qwen3-14B (same family, different generation) on FOLIO and ProofWriter, likewise confirmed ($\Delta \leq +1.47$\,pp, n.s.).

